\description{
Tests whether sample metadata, such as treatment groups, explain the
differences between samples in a distance matrix. Gives the same table as
\code{vegan::adonis2()}: degrees of freedom, sums of squares, \eqn{R^2}, and
pseudo-\eqn{F} statistics, with permutation p-values. Computes them in
parallel C, straight from the distances, without the \eqn{n \times n}
matrices that \code{adonis2()} builds for every permutation.
}
\details{
\strong{Method}

PERMANOVA (Anderson 2001) partitions the variation in the distances among
the terms of a linear model, following McArdle & Anderson (2001): with
\eqn{A} the squared distances, \eqn{G = -\frac{1}{2}JAJ} Gower's centred
matrix, and \eqn{H} the hat matrix of the (centred) model, a model's sum
of squares is \eqn{trace(HGH)}, out of a total \eqn{trace(G) =
\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i<j}d_{ij}^2}. The pseudo-\eqn{F} of a term with
\eqn{q} degrees of freedom is
\deqn{F = \frac{SS_{term} / q}{SS_{residual} / (n - 1 - p)}}
where \eqn{p} counts the degrees of freedom of the whole model. For each
permutation, the samples' distances are shuffled relative to the metadata
and \eqn{F} is recomputed. The p-value is the proportion of permutations,
counting the observed one, with an \eqn{F} at least as large:
\eqn{(1 + \#\{F_{perm} \ge F\}) / (1 + permutations)}. As in \code{adonis2()},
permuted statistics within \code{sqrt(.Machine$double.eps)} of the observed one
count as ties.

Partial models, with \code{Condition()} terms, follow \code{vegan::dbrda()} and
\code{vegan::anova.cca()}'s default "reduced" model: the conditions' effect is
removed from the distances, and the residuals are permuted.

Every term is tested with the same permutations of the (conditioned)
distances, as in \code{adonis2()}. PERMANOVA+ for PRIMER instead permutes the
residuals of a separate reduced model for each term (Freedman & Lane
1983), which can give slightly different p-values for terms after the
first.

\strong{Speed and memory}

With \eqn{Q} an orthonormal basis of the model matrix, \eqn{trace(HGH) =
\sum_c q_c^T G q_c}, and each \eqn{q^T G q = -\sum_{i<j} q_i q_j
d_{ij}^2} is read straight from \code{dm}. A permutation of the samples
permutes the rows of \eqn{Q}, so each permutation costs one pass over the
distances, shared by many vectors at once, with no \eqn{n \times n}
matrix. For a single factor, the sum of squares within groups is summed
over the pairs of samples in the same group instead (Anderson 2001), which
is faster still. Permutations run in parallel.

\strong{Reproducibility}

The permutations come from \code{seed}, not from R's random number generator,
so \code{set.seed()} has no effect, and each permutation has a random number
stream of its own. P-values are identical from run to run and for any
number of \code{cpus}. The random permutations differ from \code{adonis2()}'s, so
p-values agree with it only up to sampling error, unless the same
\code{permutations} matrix is given to both.

\strong{Differences from adonis2()}
\itemize{
\item \code{permanova()} takes distances, not a community table: compute them first
with \code{\link[=beta_div]{beta_div()}} or a metric function such as \code{\link[=bray]{bray()}}. One variable
can be given as \code{groups}, like ecodive's other tests; several as
\code{formula}, with \code{data}. \code{adonis2()}'s style, \code{permanova(dm ~ terms, data)}, also works.
\item \code{adonis2()}'s table is the \code{table} field, and its permuted \eqn{F}
statistics (\code{F.perm}) are \code{attr(res$table, 'f_perm')}.
\item A term with no degrees of freedom left (aliased with the terms before
it) has no \eqn{F} or p-value (\code{NA}), where \code{adonis2()} reports an
infinite or undefined \eqn{F}.
\item \code{strata} permutes freely within strata. For other restricted designs,
pass a \code{permutations} matrix from \code{permute::shuffleSet()}.
\item When there are fewer possible permutations than requested, \code{adonis2()}
(via \code{permute}) runs each of them once instead. \code{permanova()} always
draws random permutations.
}
}
\examples{
    # 30 samples in three treatment groups. The 'high' group has more
    # of the first five genera.
    set.seed(1)
    counts <- matrix(rpois(30 * 20, 5), 30, 20)
    rownames(counts) <- paste0('S', 1:30)
    meta <- data.frame(
      row.names = rownames(counts),
      treatment = rep(c('ctrl', 'low', 'high'), each = 10),
      ph        = round(rnorm(30, 7, 0.3), 1) )
    high <- meta$treatment == 'high'
    counts[high, 1:5] <- counts[high, 1:5] + 5

    dm <- bray(counts)

    # Does treatment explain differences in community composition?
    res <- permanova(dm, 'treatment', meta)
    res

    # The same, with the groups as a vector.
    permanova(dm, meta$treatment)

    # F, p-value and z-score as one row, and adonis2()'s table.
    as.data.frame(res)
    res$table

    # Several variables: a formula. Each term in turn, after the ones
    # before it.
    permanova(dm, data = meta, formula = ~ treatment + ph, by = 'terms')

    # The same, in adonis2()'s style.
    permanova(dm ~ treatment + ph, meta, by = 'terms')

    # pH, after removing the effect of treatment.
    permanova(dm, data = meta, formula = ~ ph + Condition(treatment))

}
